  {
    n: 14,
    name: 'Loom',
    customer: 'The Logo Wall',
    product: 'Async video messaging.',
    url: 'loom.com/customers',
    why: "Loom has earned more affection than almost any other B2B SaaS product of the last decade. 14 million users. A $1.53B Atlassian acquisition. Foundation's case study on Loom's product-led GTM, written by an outside party, is more thorough than anything Loom has published about itself.",
    flaw: "The /customers page is a logo wall and a short rotation of quotes. No quantified outcome on the front page. No individual case study with a named exec, a named team, and a before/after metric you can hold the next call accountable to. Loom has the customer love and almost none of the published proof of it. The product was so good it covered for the case studies for years. Post-acquisition, that's a luxury the page is still living off. If the only proof on a /customers page is logos, you've handed the differentiating work to your competitor's case studies.",
    criteria: [
      { label: 'Named customer', score: 'Partial' },
      { label: 'Quantified, dated outcome', score: '✗' },
      { label: 'Transferable use case', score: '✓' },
      { label: 'Narrative arc', score: '✗' },
      { label: 'Layered proof', score: '✗' },
    ],
  },
  {
    n: 15,
    name: 'Linear',
    customer: "The One That Isn't There",
    product: 'Issue tracking and project management for software teams.',
    url: "doesn't exist",
    why: "Linear doesn't have a customer stories page. No /customers, no /case-studies, no /testimonials index. The closest the company gets is a docs page about its Customer Requests feature, which is (hilariously) a feature for their customers to manage their own customer requests. Linear has, instead, accumulated a roster of unsolicited reverence from the dev tools community. Lenny Rachitsky writes about how Linear builds product. Karri Saarinen's essays on launching multiple times get cited regularly. Radix Primitives lists Linear as a case study on <em>its</em> site.",
    flaw: 'Linear can get away with this. Almost nobody else can. The replacement for an absent /customers page is community-driven advocacy that takes years to earn, and most B2B SaaS companies trying to imitate the <em>"we don\'t need case studies, the product speaks for itself"</em> energy are not Linear. They\'re a Series B company with a logo wall and a hope. The 26% of B2B deals that die for evidence-related reasons applies to every company that hasn\'t built the cultural capital Linear has. The instructive lesson is the inverse of the obvious one: Linear\'s absence is a flex earned over years of public engineering culture. Don\'t try the move unless you\'ve earned it.',
    criteria: [
      { label: 'Named customer', score: '✗' },
      { label: 'Quantified, dated outcome', score: '✗' },
      { label: 'Transferable use case', score: '✗' },
      { label: 'Narrative arc', score: '✗' },
      { label: 'Layered proof', score: '✗ (and yet)' },
